# referenceSystem v2.0 - build artefacts and local state.
#
# EVERYTHING LISTED HERE IS PRODUCED BY setupReferenceSystem AND MUST NOT BE
# COMMITTED. The MEX gateway is platform-specific, so a committed binary is
# wrong for most of the people who clone the repository and, worse, is wrong
# SILENTLY on the platform it was built for once the C sources move on. Setup
# compares the sources against the binary and rebuilds when they are newer,
# which only works if the binary is local.

# Compiled MEX gateway, one extension per platform
+referenceSystem/private/mexGateway.mex*
+referenceSystem/private/*.o
+referenceSystem/private/*.obj

# Standalone C test binaries, built by test/c/runStandaloneTests.sh. The same
# rule as the gateway for the same reason: they are platform-specific ELF or
# PE executables, wrong for most machines and SILENTLY stale on the one that
# built them once the C sources move on. Rev. 1.183's archive shipped five of
# them - 2.2 MB of Linux binaries in a two-megabyte toolbox - against this
# file's own opening paragraph (finding C-4 of the rev. 1.184 review). The
# runner rebuilds them from source in seconds; nothing reads them from disk.
test/c/build/

# Help browser search database, rebuilt by referenceSystem.buildDocumentation
doc/helpsearch*/

# Test output. The report is a deliverable of a RUN, not of the repository:
# committing one invites reading a stale result as a current one.
referenceSystem_testReport.txt
test/*_testReport.txt

# Earth orientation and leap-second files a session may download. These are
# measured data that the IERS reissues; pin a copy deliberately if a result
# has to be reproducible, and say so in the handover when you do.
data/*.txt
data/*.dat

# SPICE kernels for P21-P23. THESE ARE HUNDREDS OF MEGABYTES: sb441-n16.bsp
# alone is about 650 MB and de441.bsp about 2.6 GB. They are third-party data
# with a canonical download location (handover section H.7.1), they are not
# ours to redistribute, and one of them committed here would make the hand-over
# archive unusable - the whole toolbox is about two megabytes. Download them
# into data/ and let git ignore them; record the filename and a digest in the
# result, not the file itself.
data/*.bsp
data/*.bpc
data/*.tpc
data/*.tf
data/*.tls
data/*.tm
!data/README.md

# MATLAB and editor leavings
*.asv
*.m~
slprj/
.DS_Store

# Scratch payloads written while driving the GitHub API from a session's
# container. These were meant to live outside the tree and one of them -
# pp5.json, a pull-request body - was swept in by `git add -A` at rev. 1.210
# and survived a merge, because nothing looked at the repository ROOT. The
# ignore is the cheap half of the repair; structure/unexpectedRootFile is the
# half that would have caught it.
pp*.json
pr*.md
body*.md
*.payload.json
